(Bug #75: a list appended to through a `list` parameter stopped at the
 capacity its literal happened to allocate - max(8, element count) - and
 every append past that was dropped, because `_list_append`'s new pointer
 was stored only into the parameter's own slot. The caller's variable now
 receives it, so the answer is the initial count plus the appends and the
 literal's capacity no longer leaks into the language.)

To 'add one to' with a list called items.
    append "x" to items.

(The reported shape: one element, twenty calls, past the default capacity of 8.)
a list called xs is ["a"].
a number called n is 0.
While n is less than 20,
    'add one to' of xs,
    Set n to n add 1.
Print "one element, 20 calls: {xs's length}".

(The rows of the capacity table, each grown by the same forty calls. Before
 the fix every one of these answered max(8, element count).)
a list called 'from empty' is [].
a list called 'from three' is [1, 2, 3].
a list called 'from eight' is [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8].
a list called 'from nine' is [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9].
a number called count is 0.
While count is less than 40,
    'add one to' of 'from empty',
    'add one to' of 'from three',
    'add one to' of 'from eight',
    'add one to' of 'from nine',
    Set count to count add 1.
Print "from empty: {'from empty''s length}".
Print "from three: {'from three''s length}".
Print "from eight: {'from eight''s length}".
Print "from nine: {'from nine''s length}".

(The growth is real data, not just a count: the appended elements read back.)
Print "first: {xs's first}".
Print "last: {xs's last}".
Print "element 9: {element 9 of xs}".

(Two lists through one call keep their own storage.)
To 'add one to each of' with a list called left and a list called right.
    append "L" to left,
    append "R" to right.

a list called ones is ["one"].
a list called twos is ["two"].
a number called turn is 0.
While turn is less than 12,
    'add one to each of' of ones and twos,
    Set turn to turn add 1.
Print "two parameters: {ones's length} and {twos's length}".
Print "kept apart: {ones's last} and {twos's last}".

(A collection argument with no variable behind it still arrives intact -
 there is nothing to store back into, and nothing crashes.)
To 'count of' with a list called items. Return a number, items's length.
Print "literal argument: {'count of' of [1, 2, 3]}".
